se two trace elements, we also found a high levels of one of the trace elements As, Cd, and Ni (over 73%) in the breast milk samples. In 40% of breat milk samples, the levels of Cr, Pb, Cd, As, and Ni were all above WHO tolerable daily intake. A summary of literature findings on concentration levels of toxic metals in the breast milk samples is presented in Table 3. The consistent presence of the selected toxic metals indicates the risk of contaminantion in the breast milk of lactating mothers which may have negative effects on the child’s health. Infants are known Scientific Reports | Vol:.(1

