or consumers. Vegetables are a major component of the diet and contribute significantly to the supply of vitamins, minerals, and dietary fibers [3], however, the concentration of various pollutants, including heavy metals, can have a negative impact on consumer health due to the increase in the pollutants emission into the environment in recent decades [38, 39]. Calculation of HQ is a method for determining the presence of health risks resulting from consumption of contaminated vegetables [36]. Calculated HQ based on the concentration of heavy metals in cucumber and carrot samples is shown in Table 2. The data in Table 2 were calculated considering the average concentration of heavy metals and based on a weight of 70.7 kg for adults and 32.9 kg for children [52]. In HQ calculations, the ingestion rates for cucumber and carrot were 0.020 and 0.0052 kg day− 1, respectively [36]. Thus, per capita consumption balances high contamination because carrot has more contamination but lower intake. The difference in EDI values ​​in this study that led to difference HQ was related to the different concentrations of heavy metals in the analyzed samples. HQ was used as a parameter to identify the health risk from vegetable consumption, which is directly affected by the initial heavy metal concentration and per capita vegetable consumption [52]. Therefore, the calculated EDI for the same metals was higher in cucumber than in carrots, and the calculated EDI for chromium was higher than for other studied heavy metals. Also, lower RfD increased the calculated HQ for nickel and lead compared to other metals. The calculated HI resulting from the concentrations of detected heavy metals in carrot and cucumber samples in children and adults was less than 1, indicating no significant health risk [39]. However, the results of the carcinogenic risk assessment from carrot and cucumber consumption analyzed in this study are shown in Figs. 3 and 4. Page 7 of 13 Child Adult HI Child Adult Pb Cd 