s will form the HI, which if higher than 1 indicates the risk of non-cancerous diseases [39]. HQ = EDI/RfD (2) HI = ∑ HQ(3) The carcinogenic risk of the studied heavy metals in carrots and cucumbers was assessed by calculating the Excess Lifetime Cancer Risk (ELCR) using Eq. 4 [39]. In Eq. 4 CSF is the cancer slope factor. CSF for Pb, Cd, Cr, and Ni is 6.3, 0.0085, 0.5, 0.91, respectively Page 4 of 13 Mohammadi et al. Discover Sustainability (2025) 6:966 Page 5 of 13 [36, 40, 41]. If the ELCR is calculated to be less than 10− 6, it indicates a negligible risk, while the acceptable range for ELCR is 10− 6-10− 4, and if the ELCR is higher than 10− 4, it indicates a significant carcinogenic risk [39, 42]. Risk assessment was performed with 1000 replicates and based on the log-normal distribution of metal concentration data using Monte Carlo simulation in Excel. ELCR = EDI × CSF(4) The health risk was estimated using Monte Carlo simulation with 1000 iterations. Input parameters included the concentration of studied heavy metals, ingestion rate, exposure frequency, exposure duration, bodyweight, average time exposure, and reference dose. Most of the parameters had a log-normal distribution. 3 Results and discussion The detected concentrations of heavy metals studied in carrots and cucumbers sampled at the sampling points are shown in Table 1. The average concentrations of Pb, Cd, Cr, and Ni in the studied carrot samples were 0.303, 0.004, 0.654, and 0.409 mg kg− 1, respectively. While the concentrations of Pb, Cd, Cr, and Ni in the studied cucumber samples were 0.075, 0.003, 0.175, and 0.136 mg kg− 1, respectively. So, the order of the studied metals in carrots and cucumber was Cr > Ni > Pb > Cd. As shown in Fig. 2, the concentrations of the studied metals were not the same in the different samples. The total concentration of the studied metals in the carrot samples was 1.296–1.483 mg kg− 1, while the total concentration of the studied metals in the cucumber samples was 0.343–0.474 mg kg− 1. Also, the highest concentration of total studied heavy metals in carrot and cucumber samples was observed in SP2 and SP1, respectively. The results showed that the average concentration of the total metals studied in carrots and cucumbers was 1.37 and 0.39 mg kg− 1, respectively, indicating that the concentration of heavy metals in the root vegetables was 3.51 times higher than in the fruit vegetables. The concentration of heavy metals in the studied carrot and cucumber samples was higher than the standard in some cases. The standard of Pb concentration in cucumber and

