l Sciences, Shiraz, Iran 3 Student Research Committee, Ahvaz Jundishapur University of Medical Sciences, Ahvaz, Iran 4 Department of Public Health, School of Health, Fasa University of Medical Sciences, Fasa, Iran 5 Department of Environmental Health Engineering, School of Health, Isfahan University of Medical Sciences, Isfahan, Iran Abstract Carrots and cucumbers are highly consumed root and fruit vegetables that constitute a significant portion of the diet. However, pollutants such as heavy metals in these vegetables can lead to health consequences for consumers. In this study, heavy metal concentrations in carrots and cucumbers in southern Iran were analyzed, and the carcinogenic and non-carcinogenic risks from their consumption were assessed using Monte Carlo simulation. The results showed that the average concentrations of chromium, cadmium, nickel, and lead in cucumber samples (0.175, 0.003, 0.136, and 0.075 mg kg− 1) were lower than those in carrots (0.654, 0.004, 0.409, and 0.303 mg kg− 1). Hazard Index (HI) due to the concentrations of the studied heavy metals in cucumber and carrots were 2.31E-3 and 8.2E-3 respectively. The order of metals effective in Hazard Quotient (HQ) in studied vegetables were Pb > Ni > Cd > Cr. The Estimated Daily Intake (EDI) of the total studied metals resulting from the consumption of cucumber and carrot was 1.1 and 2.2, respectively. Therefore, the calculated carcinogenic risk based on the Excess Lifetime Cancer Risk (ELCR) associated with cucumber and carrot consumption was 2.32E-5 and 1.00E-4, respectively. Based on results, the concentrations of studied heavy metals in cucumbers and carrots may lead to potential long-term health concerns if not controlled in a plan to identify sources of emissions and reduce these pollutants in agricultural land and water. Keywords Risk assessment, Heavy metals, Monte carlo simulation, Food safety, Trace elements 1 Introduction Vegetables are a major source of vitamins, minerals, dietary fiber, and phytochemicals such as bioactive peptides, flavonoids, and phenolic compounds, which constitute an important portion of the diet in various cultures [1, 2]. Therefore, vegetables play an important role in nutrition all over the world, and their consumption has received more attention in recent decades due to the trend towards healthy lifestyle [3, 4]. In addition, the properties of vegetables and their health benefits is considered and international © The Author(s) 2025. Open Access This article is licensed under a Creative Commons Attribution-NonCommercial-NoDerivatives 4.0 Internation

-- ug --
n growth, and urbanization [11–13]. Therefore, the increase in sources of land and water pollution in recent decades and the increase in per capita consumption of vegetables as one of the main agricultural products in developing countries can have health consequences resulting from the consumption of contaminated vegetables. Heavy metals are one of the environmental concerns arising from pollution emission sources that have various health consequences for humans [14, 15]. Heavy metal concentrations in water sources are a serious concern that has been reported in many surface waters [16]. Although exposure to heavy metals has been reported even through inhalation of particulate matter in the air [17], concentrations of heavy metals in water can cause serious exposure through the food chain. In this situation, the contamination reported in soil in several studies [18, 19] could exacerbate the concentration of heavy metals in the food chain. Some heavy metals such as iron, zinc, copper, and magnesium are known as micronutrients in minor concentrations that must be supplied in the diet [20]. However, increasing the concentration of this group of heavy metals that called essential metals, in food and exposure to them above the standards recommended by international organizations such as the World Health Organization can lead to related diseases [21]. But some heavy metals, called non-essential metals, such as chromium, cadmium, lead, and mercury, can also pose a health risk to humans even in low concentrations [22]. In addition to the toxicity of heavy metals and their health consequences, the properties of these elements, including their persistence and bioaccumulation in plants, seafood, animals, and the food chain, are also important [23, 24]. So, heavy metals are of major concern regarding nutrition-related health risks due to their proven health consequences and accumulation in agricultural products, including vegetables [25]. The concentration of heavy metals in agricultural products, including vegetables, depends on the concentration of pollutants in soil and water, as well as the type of vegetables [26, 27]. In recent decades, industrial development and urbanization have been factors affecting the increase in the emission of various pollutants, including heavy metals [28]. Especially in developing countries, several studies have reported the concentration of heavy metals in soil and water [29, 30], which can be considered as a risk of Page 2 of 13 Mohammadi et al. Discover Sustainability (2025) 6:966 increasing these pollutants in agricultural product

